00:24:15.190 --> 00:24:59.790
<v Bob>Then he started to kind of needle me. I remember one thing that he did was there was this guy, Mike, in my class. And Mike was what we called in my hometown a scummer, which meant that he was on welfare. He perhaps didn't have a pair of pants for every day of the week. He was very smart, which was a strike against him at that age. And he just didn't fit in. He was a real geek. So Dave organized this fight between Mike and I-- a fist fight to prove who was the bigger faggot. Whoever would lose was the bigger faggot.
<v Bob>然后他开始找茬。我记得他做过的一件事是，班上有个叫迈克的家伙。迈克在我们家乡被称为“社会底层”（scummer），意味着他领救济金。他可能连每天换洗的裤子都没有。他很聪明，但在那个年纪这反而是个缺点。他不合群。他是个真正的怪胎。所以戴夫组织了我和迈克之间的一场架——通过打拳来证明谁才是那个更大的“娘炮”。输的人就是更大的那个。

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:24.960
<v Bob>And he positioned us-- he spun it so there was no way I could not fight. If I didn't have this fight, then things would just get worse for me. Things would get worse. Mike was at the bottom of the barrel, and I was there with him. And Dave had to prove that I was below him. And so he organized this whole fight. And everyone in the class was like, "Are you going to fight Mike? Are you going to fight Mike?"
<v Bob>他设好局，让我根本没法不打。如果我不打，情况只会更糟。迈克本来就在社会底层，我当时也和他一起在那儿。戴夫必须证明我比他更低。于是他组织了那场架。班上每个人都在说：“你会打迈克吗？你会打迈克吗？”

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:47.220
<v Bob>And I finally conceded. I conceded to fight him. Dave organized the whole event. It was to happen at this playground. And we went, and he tried to get this whole audience to come and see it. Unfortunately, nobody came but him-- well, I guess, fortunately. Nobody came but him and one other person.
<v Bob>我终于让步了。我同意跟他打。戴夫组织了一切。地点在操场。我们去了，他试图让全班都来看。不幸的是，没人来——或者说幸运的是。除了他还有另一个人，没人来。

00:25:47.220 --> 00:26:15.240
<v Bob>So Mike and I went there. And neither of us wanted to fight each other. Neither of us had any reason in the world to fight each other. So I just remember standing there facing him, just laughing, just like, what are we supposed to do? We're standing here. We've been forced into this just to save our own souls. And now we have to inflict pain on each other. And we stood there for the longest time. And Dave kept trying to push us into fighting. He was saying, "Come on. Just throw a punch. Just throw a punch. Just start."
<v Bob>我和迈克到了那里。我们谁都不想打谁。我们毫无理由要打架。我记得我站在那儿对着他笑，想的是：我们到底该干什么？我们在这儿，只是被迫为了保住自己的尊严。而现在我们却要彼此伤害。我们站了很久。戴夫不停地催促我们动手。他说：“来吧。挥拳啊。挥拳啊。开始啊。”

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:41.230
<v Bob>We waited for the longest time, and then finally, I was just like, this is ridiculous. So I went up, and I shoved him. And then we started to fight. We weren't really into fighting, but we had to do it then. And I pushed him at one point, and he fell backwards and he hurt his wrist. And I got on top of him. and I held his wrist. And I remember saying to him, "Do you give up?" And he wouldn't give up. And I was holding his wrist back.
<v Bob>我们站了很久，最后我觉得：这也太荒谬了。于是我走过去推了他一下。然后我们开始打架。我们其实并不想打，但当时不得不打。打着打着，我推了他一下，他向后摔倒，手腕受伤了。我压在他身上，抓着他的手腕。我记得我对他说：“你认输吗？” 他不肯认输。我抓着他的手腕。

00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:58.740
<v Bob>And each time I kept asking if he was going to give up, and he kept saying no. And each time he'd say no, I'd bend his wrist back a little bit more. And he was crying in pain. And I kept saying, I'm going to bend it back. I'm going to bend it back. I'm going to bend it back, thinking, I don't want to break this person's wrist. I have no reason to break this person's wrist.
<v Bob>每次我问他认不认输，他都说不。每当他说不，我就把他的手腕再往后扳一点。他痛得大哭。我嘴里说着，我要扳下去了，我要扳下去了，我想的是：我不想弄断这人的手腕。我没有理由弄断他的手腕。

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:44.690
<v Bob>And I was faced at that moment with either giving up and getting kicked even more or going ahead with really hurting this person and hoping that it would save me in some way. And I broke his wrist. He came in the next day into school with a cast on. I don't know how his family paid for his cast. And everybody saw that and said, "You idiot, [? Cooze! ?] What did you do? You idiot!" And it made it even worse.
<v Bob>那一刻我面临抉择：要么认输被更严重地霸凌，要么继续伤人，希望能以此保住自己。我弄断了他的手腕。第二天他打着石膏来学校了。我不知道他家怎么付得起医药费。大家都看到了，指着我说：“你这白痴，[? Cooze !] 你干了什么？你这个白痴！” 情况变得更糟了。

00:27:44.690 --> 00:28:00.370
<v Bob>I just didn't understand. I didn't know what was going on. That's the most frustrating for me, even still today. I always thought it was me. I always thought it was something I was doing. I thought it was the way that I wore my clothes. I thought it was the shoes that I wore. I thought it was the way that I laughed. I remember.
<v Bob>我就是不明白。我不知道发生了什么。即使到了今天，这也是最让我感到挫败的地方。我一直以为是我的错。我以为是我做了什么。我以为是我穿衣服的方式。我以为是我穿的鞋子。我以为是我笑的方式。我记得。

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:32.790
<v Bob>And I remember I tried to change these things. I tried not to laugh so loud. I tried to cough differently. They made fun of the most minuscule things that are just human, like chewing. If they would've noticed the way that I breathed, they would have made fun of that. I remember I tried lifting weights. And it was impossible, because there was nothing I could do to make them change their minds. If I lifted weights, it was the way that I lifted weights. They would come up with something.
<v Bob>我也记得我曾尝试改变这些。我试过不要大声笑。我试过改变咳嗽的方式。他们嘲笑最细微的东西，比如咀嚼。如果他们注意到我呼吸的方式，他们肯定也会拿那个开玩笑。我记得我曾尝试举重。但那是徒劳的，因为无论我怎么做，都无法改变他们的看法。即使我去举重，他们也会嘲笑我举重的方式。他们总能找到理由。

00:28:32.790 --> 00:28:54.400
<v Bob>It was a no-win situation. And I guess I knew how smart he was, and I knew how well he knew me. And I guess, all of a sudden, I had thought that he had seen all these things in me that were just wrong, just so not cool.
<v Bob>这是一场必输的战斗。我猜我知道他有多聪明，我知道他有多了解我。我猜，突然间，我以为是他看透了我身上的一切缺点，那些真的不“酷”的地方。

00:28:54.400 --> 00:29:20.520
<v Bob>I remember very clearly there was a moment that the clouds just cleared and everything became possible. I went to the Pennsylvania Governor's School for the Arts in my junior year. The summer between my junior and senior year, that was when I met people who were actors, like I was, who were my age and who respected me.
<v Bob>我清晰地记得，有一刻云开雾散，一切都变得可能了。我在高三那年去了宾夕法尼亚州州长艺术学校。那是高三和大四之间的暑假，在那里我遇到了和我同龄的、尊重我的演员朋友。

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:47.410
<v Bob>And I remember very clearly, it was after lunch, and people who were in my theater class, we were going down to the cafeteria. And I had forgotten my bag in the cafeteria. And I said, "Oh, just wait for me one second. I just want to get my bag." And I went down to the cafeteria, and I got my bag. And I came back up, and they were still there waiting for me. And I remember so distinctly remembering, "Oh, my god. These people, they waited for me."
<v Bob>我记忆犹新，那是午饭后，我戏剧课的同学正一起去食堂。我把包落在食堂了。我说：“噢，等我一下，我去拿包。” 我跑下楼拿了包，回来时他们还在那等我。我记得非常清楚，当时心想：“天哪。这些人，他们等我。”

00:29:47.410 --> 00:30:05.590
<v Bob>That meant so much to me, because I felt like I'm not this idiot. I'm not this geek. I'm not a person who everyone should hate. I'm actually a pretty good human being, and people like me.
<v Bob>那对我来说意义重大，因为我感觉自己不是那个白痴。我不是个怪胎。我不是一个每个人都该憎恨的人。我实际上是一个挺好的人，人们喜欢我。

00:30:05.590 --> 00:30:24.490
<v Bob>Sometimes, I feel like there's some justice in the fact that I'm surrounded now by many people who love me and respect me. And I don't know how many people he has in his life. But the tables have turned in that way.
<v Bob>有时候，我觉得现在的我被许多爱我、尊重我的人包围，这本身就是一种正义。我不知道他生活中有多少人。但在这个意义上，形势已经扭转了。

00:30:24.490 --> 00:31:20.850
<v Bob>And when I go back home and I see people looking at me the way that they looked at me in high school, I think there is some justice in this, because those people are probably-- some of those people, I've heard, are in bad situations with their families. They've got wives they don't love. They've been through divorces. They don't have any friends. They're in the same bar they were in four or five years ago, drinking the same beer they drank. And their gut is a little bigger, and their nose is a little redder. And they just look tired. And I feel like my life is in pretty good shape, and I'm happy. And that's the sweetest revenge. And last Christmas, I ran into him at a mall. And he looked more jaundiced and more sickly than ever.
<v Bob>当我回到家乡，看到人们用高中时代那种眼光看我时，我觉得这就是正义，因为那些人……我听说有些人过得很糟。他们和不爱的妻子在一起。他们离过婚。没朋友。他们还在四五年前去的酒吧喝着同样的啤酒。肚子大了一点，鼻子红了一点。他们看起来只是很累。我觉得我的生活状态很不错，我很快乐。这就是最甜蜜的复仇。去年圣诞节，我在商场遇到了他。他看起来比以往任何时候都更加面色蜡黄、病态。

00:31:20.850 --> 00:31:42.140
<v Ira Glass>Bob says that it was because Dave made him an outcast in junior high school that he ended up spending a lot of time with the theater teacher. Because the other kids wouldn't talk to him. And this teacher put him in plays and took him to plays in New York City, showing him that there was a bigger world outside their hometown.
<v Ira Glass>鲍勃说，正是因为戴夫在初中时把他变成了边缘人，他才花了很多时间与戏剧老师待在一起。因为其他孩子不跟他说话。这位老师让他参与演出，并带他去纽约市看剧，向他展示了家乡以外更广阔的世界。

00:31:42.140 --> 00:32:03.800
<v Bob>If it weren't for Dave, I would not be doing what I'm doing right now. There's no way. I don't know what I would have been doing, but because of what he did to me, I found theater, and it's been a really good life so far.
<v Bob>如果不是戴夫，我现在绝对不会做我正在做的事。没可能。我不知道我会做什么，但因为他对我做的一切，我发现了戏剧，到目前为止，那是一段非常美好的人生。

00:32:03.800 --> 00:33:02.880
<v Ira Glass>Bob's a director in Manhattan. Dave gives his version of the story in Act Three of Your Radio Playhouse .
