<v Ira Glass>Well, Act Three, Dave's Response. It's an odd thing, actually, as a reporter, calling a stranger on the phone and asking him to defend things he did 15 years before as a teenager. Who amongst us wants to get that call? So I reached Dave at his parents' house, where he still lives. And he's a devout Christian now. He writes and edits a Christian magazine for young people. And he says he's never been happier.
<v Ira Glass>好了，第三幕：戴夫的回应。作为记者，给一个陌生人打电话，要求他为 15 年前青春期时做过的事辩护，这其实挺奇怪的。我们谁想接到那样的电话呢？所以我联系到了住在父母家的戴夫，他至今仍住在那里。他现在是一位虔诚的基督徒。他为年轻人撰写和编辑一本基督教杂志。他说他从未如此快乐。

00:37:17.610 --> 00:37:40.110
<v Ira Glass>No, he does not really think about Bob very often. No, Bob is not a big figure in his life. No, he does not remember the fight with Mike. Yes, he remembers being enemies with Bob in the seventh grade. But no, he wasn't sure why. The best that he could remember, Bob was to blame. Bob, by becoming friends with someone else.
<v Ira Glass>不，他确实不常想起鲍勃。不，鲍勃在他生活中不是个重要人物。不，他不记得和迈克的那场架了。是的，他记得七年级时和鲍勃成了敌人。但不知道为什么。他能记起的最好说法是，鲍勃有错。鲍勃因为和别人成了朋友。

00:37:40.110 --> 00:37:52.690
<v Dave>And they were doing these other things. I got really mad and everything, and it was like going from best friends to worst. I'm trying to remember this.
<v Dave>而且他们还在做其他那些事。我变得非常生气，一切就那样了，感觉从最好的朋友变成了最坏的敌人。我正在努力回忆这件事。

00:37:52.690 --> 00:38:07.690
<v Ira Glass>And he remembers-- is this also accurate or do you remember this as well? He remembers you turning other people against him. He says that at one point, there was actually a "I Hate [? Cooze ?] Club" that you started.
<v Ira Glass>他还记得——这准确吗？还是你也记得？他记得你煽动其他人反对他。他说有一次，你甚至组建了一个“我恨 [? Cooze ?] 俱乐部”。

00:38:07.690 --> 00:38:25.400
<v Dave>Oh, gosh. Again, I don't even remember that. But now that you say it, gosh, what a terrible person I must've been, because that's horrible. Oh, man, I actually kind of remember that now. That's pretty lame.
<v Dave>噢，天哪。真的，我不记得了。但你现在一提，天哪，我当时一定是个多糟糕的人啊，因为那太可怕了。噢，天哪，我其实确实有点想起来了。那太逊了。

00:38:25.400 --> 00:38:27.000
<v Ira Glass>What do you remember of it?
<v Ira Glass>你记得什么吗？

00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:38.070
<v Dave>I don't. That's just it. When you said the name, it's like, oh, my gosh, did I really do that? I have no idea.
<v Dave>我不记得了。就是这样。当你提到名字时，就像：噢，天哪，我真的做了那样的事吗？我完全不知道。

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:45.960
<v Ira Glass>When he describes you during that period, he felt like you had a kind of David Koresh-like power over others.
<v Ira Glass>当他描述你那段时期的表现时，他觉得你当时对其他人拥有那种大卫·考雷什般的力量。

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:50.490
<v Dave>Well, I mean, we were in high school. I'm trying to think of--
<v Dave>嗯，我是说，我们那时候在高中。我正试着想——

00:38:50.490 --> 00:38:53.880
<v Ira Glass>Can you imagine yourself being seen in that light?
<v Ira Glass>你能想象自己被那样看待吗？

00:38:53.880 --> 00:39:19.510
<v Dave>No, not really at all. Looking back now, I feel like I was always the one that was really, totally at the bottom of the social level, or whatever you want to call it, in school. And I'm thinking, I wonder what caused me to do something like that. Obviously, to make myself look better or something, because-- I don't know, but that's pretty bad.
<v Dave>不，一点也不。现在回过头看，我觉得我当时在学校里一直是在社交层面的最底层，或者随便你怎么叫。我在想，到底是什么导致我做出那种事。显然是为了让自己看起来更好什么的，因为——我不知道，但这真的很糟糕。

00:39:19.510 --> 00:39:53.490
<v Ira Glass>I'll tell you the way that Bob remembers and interprets what happened between the two of you. He doesn't remember it as being the kind of thing where he became friends with somebody else. He says that you guys were really, really close-- really close friends. And then, when everybody hit adolescence, a really typical thing happened to you, and that is that other boys started accusing you of being gay. And that you publicly wanted to put a stop to it.
<v Ira Glass>我来告诉你鲍勃是怎么记忆和解读你们之间发生的事情的。他并不觉得是因为他结交了别人。他说你们当时关系非常、非常亲密。然后，当大家都进入青春期时，一件非常典型的事情发生在了你们身上，那就是其他男孩开始指责你们是同性恋。而你为了公开表明立场，想制止这种指责。

00:39:53.490 --> 00:40:18.650
<v Dave>Gosh, honestly, I cannot even remember this. He has a much better memory than me. I know we were close and everything, but I don't ever remember any accusations like that. No. And I'm trying to think, too. I'm sitting here trying to drag it out of my memory as to why did we start fighting in the first place? And I'm thinking, but jeez.
<v Dave>天哪，说真的，我甚至完全想不起来了。他的记忆力比我好。我知道我们当时很亲密，但我完全不记得有那样的指责。没有。我还在试着想，我坐在这儿拼命搜寻记忆，到底我们为什么会打起来？我心想，天哪。

00:40:18.650 --> 00:40:38.490
<v Ira Glass>Actually, the thing that it's making me think about is, in the case of a story like this, I think it's easier for a person who was the injured person-- or believes they were the injured person-- to remember something than the person who did something hurtful. Because I think it's so much more embarrassing to remember yourself as being someone who did something hurtful.
<v Ira Glass>其实这让我想到一点，像这样的故事，我觉得受伤的一方——或者认为自己是受害方的一方——更容易记得这些事，而不是做伤害行为的人。因为我觉得记住自己曾做过伤害人的事更令人难堪。

00:40:38.490 --> 00:41:15.800
<v Dave>Right. Now I work with kids that are about that age. And they get in their little spats sometimes. And we just always go to the Bible together. And I try to say, well, look, this person's your friend. We just had an incident like this last week. This one girl, her friends, all of a sudden, just wouldn't talk to her. I felt really bad for this person because she's always one of the gang. I'm thinking, man, I've got to get a hold of her and see what's wrong and everything and get all these people back together, because this isn't good.
<v Dave>没错。我现在和那个年纪的孩子一起工作。他们有时会闹点小别扭。我们总是回到圣经中寻找答案。我总是尝试说：看，那个人是你的朋友。我们上周刚发生过类似的事情。有个女孩，她的朋友们突然就不理她了。我当时很为她难过，因为她一直是那个圈子的一员。我想：天哪，我得找她聊聊看看出了什么问题，然后把这些人都召集起来，因为这样可不好。

00:41:15.800 --> 00:41:19.960
<v Dave>[TELEPHONE RINGING]
<v Dave>[电话铃响]

00:41:19.960 --> 00:41:20.830
<v Bob>Hello?
<v Bob>喂？

00:41:20.830 --> 00:41:22.170
<v Ira Glass>Hi, is Bob there?
<v Ira Glass>你好，鲍勃在吗？

00:41:22.170 --> 00:41:23.800
<v Bob>This is Bob.
<v Bob>我是鲍勃。

00:41:23.800 --> 00:41:29.120
<v Ira Glass>So after I spoke with Dave, I called Bob. I guess you could figure that out for yourself, huh?
<v Ira Glass>所以和戴夫谈完后，我给鲍勃打了电话。我想你自己也能猜到吧，哈？

00:41:29.120 --> 00:41:41.610
<v Ira Glass>You describe him going and whispering in people's ear, Iago-like, saying, go do this, go do that, walk up to Bob and say this to him, push him in the locker. And he remembers none of that.
<v Ira Glass>你描述他当时像伊阿古一样在别人耳边细语，说：去干这个，去干那个，走到鲍勃面前说这个，把他推进储物柜。而他完全不记得了。

00:41:41.610 --> 00:41:43.250
<v Bob>I remember that very clearly.
<v Bob>我记得非常清楚。

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:47.510
<v Ira Glass>Well, he remembers none of it.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，他完全不记得了。

00:41:47.510 --> 00:41:59.200
<v Bob>I remember it very clearly. That is stuff that scarred me for years and years and years. And it's no surprise that I remember it and he doesn't.
<v Bob>我记得非常清楚。那是折磨我多年、多年、多年的事。我不奇怪我记得而他不记得。

00:41:59.200 --> 00:42:15.420
<v Ira Glass>Thinking about what happened to you, just thinking about it over the last few days, it's really made me think about what it means to love somebody. You know how when you love somebody, you're always more aware of what they do in the room than anybody else?
<v Ira Glass>想想发生在你身上的事，在这过去几天里一直思考，这让我不得不思考“爱一个人”意味着什么。你知道当你爱一个人时，你总是比其他人更在意他在屋里的一举一动吗？

00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:33.340
<v Ira Glass>And because you guys really loved each other, anything that he would do in a room would be something that would be more apparent to you than what anybody else was doing and would seem more important. So if somebody else were just as influential over other kids in the class, you would always be more aware of what Dave did.
<v Ira Glass>因为你们曾真心相爱，他在屋里做的任何事都会比别人做的事更引起你的注意，显得更重要。所以如果其他人对班上的学生也同样有影响力，你也会比别人更在意戴夫所做的事。

00:42:33.340 --> 00:43:03.570
<v Bob>Yeah, I would say all of his activities were illuminated more to me than to anyone else because of that. But I do know that he was the focal point for all this antagonism against me throughout those years. No doubt. He generated it. It didn't just come from nowhere.
<v Bob>没错，我想他所有的活动在我眼中确实比在别人眼里更显眼，正是因为那个原因。但我确实知道，在那几年里，他就是所有针对我敌对活动的焦点。毫无疑问。是他制造了这一切。这并不是凭空发生的。

00:43:03.570 --> 00:43:34.010
<v Ira Glass>It's an odd thing to have a profound experience with somebody else, a really intimate experience, and you remember every detail. And the other person remembers none of it. And so you're left there alone. When I talked to Bob, I asked him about this over and over and over again because I thought it was such a particular kind of moment for him. But he didn't really take it that way.
<v Ira Glass>和一个人的深层经历确实是很奇怪的，一段非常亲密的经历，你记得每一个细节。而对方却什么都不记得。于是你就只能独自留在那里。和鲍勃谈话时，我一遍又一遍地问他这件事，因为我觉得这对当时的他来说是一个极其特别的时刻。但他并不完全是那样看的。

00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:45.590
<v Bob>I don't know, I have no reason to feel lonely about this. I don't expect Dave to remember it. What you're saying sounds like I should have animosity toward him because of what he did to me.
<v Bob>我不知道，我没理由在这件事上感到孤独。我不指望戴夫还记得。你说的听起来好像我因为他对我做过的事而对他怀恨在心似的。

00:43:45.590 --> 00:43:55.450
<v Ira Glass>No, I don't mean animosity at all. Actually, in fact, just the opposite. Simply someone else to confirm that it happened. That it happened and that it had a meaning.
<v Ira Glass>不，我完全没提到怨恨。事实上，恰恰相反。只是需要有人来确认这件事确实发生过。发生过，并且它是有意义的。

00:43:55.450 --> 00:44:27.170
<v Bob>Yeah. Yeah, you may be right. Now I feel alone. [LAUGHS] I mean, yeah, I can see myself saying, "But, Dave, don't you remember?" This was a massive event in my life.
<v Bob>是啊。是啊，你可能是对的。现在我感到孤独了。[笑] 我的意思是，是啊，我能想象自己说：“但是，戴夫，你不记得了吗？” 那是我生命中的重大事件。

00:44:27.170 --> 00:44:54.600
<v Ira Glass>As you were talking, I was thinking this is the Gabriel Garcia Marquez part of the story where, like, is memory lost? Does memory last? Does it mean anything if an entire nation forgets what it was? Or does it mean anything if we are the only one who remembers an experience and then we are lost, and so that experience is never remembered? Does it mean anything? And you're saying, really, no. Maybe it meant something to me, but no, not really.
<v Ira Glass>当你说话时，我正在想这是加西亚·马尔克斯式的故事——记忆会消失吗？记忆会长存吗？如果一个国家彻底遗忘了它的过去，那还有意义吗？如果我是唯一记得这段经历的人，那我迷失了，这段经历就从未被记得吗？这有意义吗？你刚才在说，其实，没有。也许这对我来说有意义，但真的，没有意义。

00:44:54.600 --> 00:45:27.900
<v Bob>And I can assure you that I am like-- I'm going to pronounce his name wrong, it's Jorge Luis Borges. Like he says, I'm remembering the last time I remembered it, which was a memory of the last time I remembered it, which is a memory of the last time I remembered it. It increasingly gets more and more dramatic, and the moment gets refined down to-- it gets distilled down into something which becomes very palatable and very understandable.
<v Bob>我可以向你保证，我就像——发音可能会错，是豪尔赫·路易斯·博尔赫斯。正如他所说，我是在记忆我最后一次记住它的时候，那是我最后一次记忆时的记忆，是我最后一次记忆时的记忆。它变得越来越戏剧化，那一刻被提炼，被精炼成某种变得非常可口和非常容易理解的东西。

00:45:27.900 --> 00:45:47.630
<v Ira Glass>One loose end in the whole thing, of course, was the friend, the mysterious friend. Remember, Dave said that Bob was to blame for them becoming enemies in the seventh grade by making friends with a guy who, we were told, is named Mike, though not the Mike whose wrist Bob later broke.
<v Ira Glass>整件事里有一个悬念，当然，就是那个朋友，那个神秘朋友。还记得吗？戴夫说鲍勃要对他们七年级时反目成仇负责，因为鲍勃和一个家伙成了朋友，也就是我们提到的迈克，尽管他不是鲍勃后来弄断手腕的那个迈克。

00:45:47.630 --> 00:46:15.150
<v Bob>I can sort of understand that. I know which Mike you're referring to, and I know that we did start to become friends then. But the friendship that I had with Dave was always the strongest friendship that I ever had up until probably five years ago when I met the person who I consider my best friend now. He always used to use the term "false friend" in seventh and eighth grade. I remember that clearly. He used to always say I was his "false friend."
<v Bob>我多少能理解。我知道你指的是哪个迈克，我也知道我们在那时开始成为了朋友。但在我遇到现在认为是最好的朋友之前的五年里，我和戴夫的友谊一直是我最深厚的。在七八年级时，他一直用“伪朋友”这个词。我记得很清楚。他总是说我是他的“伪朋友”。

00:46:15.150 --> 00:46:16.560
<v Ira Glass>Meaning what?
<v Ira Glass>这是什么意思？

00:46:16.560 --> 00:46:25.220
<v Bob>Well, what is it? I don't know. What does it mean? It means you were friends, but now it's fake or it's--
<v Bob>嗯，是什么意思呢？我不知道。那意味着什么？这意味着你们曾经是朋友，但现在是假的，或者……

00:46:25.220 --> 00:46:28.630
<v Ira Glass>See, to me, false friend means that he thinks that you betrayed him.
<v Ira Glass>你看，对我来说，“伪朋友”意味着他认为你背叛了他。

00:46:28.630 --> 00:46:46.970
<v Bob>Could be. It could be. God, wouldn't that be awful if it all boiled down to one event where he felt like me and Mike were becoming friends, and it could've been cleared up in one conversation?
<v Bob>可能是吧。可能是。天哪，如果这一切仅仅是因为某个瞬间，他觉得我和迈克成了朋友，而如果只要一场谈话就能解释清楚，那该有多糟糕。

00:46:46.970 --> 00:46:48.530
<v Ira Glass>That it was a complete misunderstanding.
<v Ira Glass>那完全是一个误解。

00:46:48.530 --> 00:46:54.090
<v Bob>That it was just a total tiny misunderstanding that could have been cleared up?
<v Bob>那完全是一个微不足道的误解，本来可以解释清楚的？

00:46:54.090 --> 00:46:57.130
<v Ira Glass>Happens all the time.
<v Ira Glass>这种事经常发生。

00:46:57.130 --> 00:47:19.280
<v Bob>Boy, I would just love to see what my life would have been like if that would have happened.
<v Bob>天哪，我真想看看如果当时解释清楚了，我的人生会是什么样。

00:47:19.280 --> 00:47:26.680
<v Ira Glass>In Act Four, another perspective on the whole thing from David Sedaris. But first, let's go to Pledge Central. Shirley Jahad.
<v Ira Glass>在第四幕，戴维·塞达里斯关于整件事的另一个视角。但在那之前，让我们去认捐中心。雪莉·贾哈德。

00:47:26.680 --> 00:47:27.560
<v Shirley Jahad>Ira Glass.
<v Shirley Jahad>艾拉·格拉斯。

00:47:27.560 --> 00:47:28.510
<v Ira Glass>Hi, honey.
<v Ira Glass>你好，亲爱的。

00:47:28.510 --> 00:47:29.730
<v Shirley Jahad>Hi, sweetie.
<v Shirley Jahad>你好，甜心。

00:47:29.730 --> 00:47:32.080
<v Ira Glass>I understand that we're having problems with the phones.
<v Ira Glass>我想我们的电话系统出了点问题。

00:47:32.080 --> 00:48:02.680
<v Shirley Jahad>We have demons, demons on the pledge lines. Maybe Bob or somebody has infiltrated here. Anyway, the number is 312-832-3160. If you're having trouble getting through, please be patient and do not give up on us. 312-832-3160 to pledge your support for Your Radio Playhouse. on WBEZ Chicago. Now, I understand some people are calling and it's ringing, but we're not hearing it ring here. And eventually we do hear it ring, but that's after several minutes.
<v Shirley Jahad>认捐热线受到了恶魔骚扰。也许是鲍勃或谁渗透进来了。总之，号码是 312-832-3160。如果你们打不进来，请保持耐心，不要放弃。312-832-3160，认捐支持 WBEZ 芝加哥的《你的广播剧场》。现在，我知道有些人打电话进来，铃声确实在响，但我们这边没听到。最终我们听到了，但那已经过了几分钟了。

00:48:02.680 --> 00:48:13.600
<v Ira Glass>So if you're having trouble getting through, and if you don't get through until the exact moment when our show ends, and you want some of the premia from this hour and the phones aren't working, ask for them and we'll give them to you.
<v Ira Glass>所以，如果你很难打进来，如果你直到节目结束的那一刻才打通，而你又想要这一个小时里的一些赠品，即使电话坏了，尽管要，我们会给你的。

00:48:13.600 --> 00:48:19.490
<v Shirley Jahad>That's right. 312-832-3160.
<v Shirley Jahad>没错。312-832-3160。

00:48:19.490 --> 00:48:42.140
<v Ira Glass>Here we go. Here's something we haven't played yet. Again, we have special premia. One is the Un-Cabaret CD. One is the Shut Up, Little Man CD, which maybe I'll just play a little clip of that real fast. It's a very odd CD-- these two guys who would fight all the time. And it's sort of audio verite recorded by their neighbors next door as these two men would scream at each other. Here we go.
<v Ira Glass>好了。这里有我们还没放过的。再说一遍，我们有特别赠品。一个是“非歌舞表演”CD，一个是《闭嘴，小矮子》CD，也许我可以快速放一点点片段。那是一张很奇怪的 CD——这两个人总是吵架。这是邻居记录下的音频实录，这两个男人对着尖叫。开始吧。

00:48:42.140 --> 00:49:04.300
<v Man>--cut your toenails, so why would I worry about it? For god's sakes, shut up, little man. I don't want to watch you cut your toenails. I would liked to have eaten tonight also, but I can't because of you talking about toenails.
<v Man>——剪你的脚趾甲，我为什么要担心？看在上帝的份上，闭嘴，小矮子。我不想看你剪脚趾甲。我本来也想吃晚饭的，但因为你一直叨叨脚趾甲，我吃不下。

00:49:04.300 --> 00:49:35.220
<v Ira Glass>Now, I should say the music in there is actually from our little mix that we did on the show. And this is one of those things that it's hard to get a sense of just in a brief little clip. Another thing we're giving away is recent work by beloved NPR commentator David Sedaris. And that includes work that he's done here on Your Radio Playhouse , that is, some of the radio plays he's done with his sister, Amy, and other actors. And this piece, this half-hour hitchhiking story that he told. Here's one little brief, brief episode from that story.
<v Ira Glass>现在，我应该说里面的音乐其实是我们节目里做的混音。这是一些很难从简短片段中体会精髓的东西。我们送出的另一个赠品是备受喜爱的 NPR 评论员戴维·塞达里斯的近期作品。其中包括他在《你的广播剧场》中做的内容，也就是他和他妹妹艾米以及其他演员录制的广播剧。还有这篇，他讲述的那个半小时搭便车的故事。这是故事里的一小段摘录。

00:49:35.220 --> 00:49:57.200
<v David Sedaris>I got an interminable ride with a pantyhose salesman who spent six hours saying "You just take and take, don't you? Don't you? Out there with your thumb in the air, not a care in the world, grabbing whatever you can get. Yes, sir, you take and you take until you're ready to burst. But what about giving? Did you ever think about that? Of course not. You're too busy taking.
<v David Sedaris>我搭上了一辆连绵不断的车，是个卖连裤袜的推销员，他花了六个小时说：“你只是索取和索取，对吧？不是吗？在那儿大拇指朝上，一点也不在乎这个世界，抓住你能得到的任何东西。没错，先生，你拿了又拿，直到准备爆掉。但给予呢？你想到过那个吗？当然没有。你太忙于索取了。”

00:49:57.200 --> 00:50:32.770
<v David Sedaris>Me, I'm what you call a taxpayer. Tax, it's a tariff that working people have to pay so that someone like you can enjoy a life of leisure. I give, and I give until I've got nothing left. Then I turn right around, and I give some more. I give and give to all of Uncle Sam's little takers. And I've been thinking that maybe it's about time I get a little something in return. Yes, indeed. Maybe it's time we try that shoe on the other foot for a change. You, my young friend, are going to wash my car, inside and out, and you're going to pay for it."
<v David Sedaris>“我呢，我是所谓的纳税人。税收，是劳动人民必须缴纳的关税，好让像你这样的人能享受闲适的生活。我给予，我一直给予，直到一无所有。然后我回过头来，再给更多。我给予，给予山姆大叔的所有小索取者。我想也许是时候让我得到点回报了。没错。也许是时候让角色互换一下了。你，我年轻的朋友，你要去给我洗车，里里外外，而且你要自己付钱。”

00:50:32.770 --> 00:50:43.010
<v Ira Glass>Anyway, again, the number if you want this very odd collection of stories from David Sedaris, 832-3160. The collection includes the story that we're going to hear at the end of this evening's show.
<v Ira Glass>不管怎样，再说一次，如果你想要这个来自戴维·塞达里斯的极其奇怪的故事集，拨打 832-3160。这个合集包含了我们今晚节目最后要听的故事。

00:50:43.010 --> 00:51:06.640
<v Shirley Jahad>312-832-3160. Call now to pledge your support for Your Radio Playhouse , a place where you hear David Sedaris' stories in their entirety, the uncut versions you will get on that cassette that you can get for a $60 pledge. 312-832-3160. Be patient, and let that phone ring, and we will get to you as soon as we can.
<v Shirley Jahad>312-832-3160。现在就打电话认捐支持《你的广播剧场》，在这里你可以听到戴维·塞达里斯故事的完整未删减版，也就是你认捐 60 美元可以得到的磁带。312-832-3160。请耐心等待，让电话响，我们会尽快接听。

00:51:06.640 --> 00:51:28.200
<v Ira Glass>We put a lot of effort into this show to bring you unusual stories that you will not hear anywhere else. And we want your support. We want your support. And we were trying to find premia that you would enjoy and could get nowhere else. So please, please call. Please call and join our fun little party here. And I guess that's it. 832-3160.
<v Ira Glass>为了给你们带来别处听不到的不寻常故事，我们在这档节目中付出了很多努力。我们想要你们的支持。我们想要你们的支持。我们试图寻找你们会喜欢且在别处找不到的赠品。所以请，请拨打电话。请拨打电话，加入我们这场有趣的派对。我想就是这样了。832-3160。

00:51:28.200 --> 00:51:33.720
<v Shirley Jahad>312-832-3160. And take it, Ira. I guess we'll go on.
<v Shirley Jahad>312-832-3160。接下去吧，艾拉。我想我们要继续了。

00:51:33.720 --> 00:52:00.210
<v Ira Glass>Well, Act Four. Act Four of this evening's program is called Another Dave. And this story by David Sedaris, frequent Morning Edition commentator, member of the Pinetree Gang, contributor to this program. And this is a story of ultimate Machiavellian scheming. It's kind of an endpoint as to how far you can go. It's called "The Last Time You'll Ever Hear from Me." It's a story, and it's read by Sarah Thyre.
<v Ira Glass>好了，第四幕。今晚节目的第四幕叫“另一个戴夫”。这个故事来自频繁出现在《早间版》的评论员、松树帮成员、本节目的贡献者戴维·塞达里斯。这是一个关于终极马基雅维利式阴谋的故事。这算是你可以走多远的一个终点。它叫《你最后一次听到我的消息》（The Last Time You'll Ever Hear from Me）。这是一个故事，由莎拉·泰尔朗读。

00:52:00.210 --> 00:52:32.160
<v Sarah Thyre>Dear friends and family, by the time you receive this letter, I will be dead. Those of you attending this service are sitting quietly holding a beautiful paperweight, a gift from the collection which, in life, had been my pride and joy. You turn the paperweight over in your hands, look deep inside at the object embedded in the glass, be it a rose or a scorpion, whatever. And through your tears, you ask, what is death like?
<v Sarah Thyre>亲爱的朋友和家人，当我这封信传到你们手中时，我已经死了。那些参加追悼会的人正静静地坐着，手里拿着一个精美的镇纸，那是从我生前引以为傲的收藏品中挑选的一份礼物。你们在手中翻转着镇纸，注视着嵌入玻璃深处的物体，无论是玫瑰还是蝎子，随便什么。在泪水中，你们问，死亡是什么样的？

00:52:32.160 --> 00:52:48.570
<v Sarah Thyre>By this time, I certainly know the answer to that question, but I'm unable to give details. Know only that I will one day meet you upon the grassy plains of heaven where, with the exception of Randy Sykes and Annette Kelper, I will be tickled to embrace you and catch up on all the news.
<v Sarah Thyre>到那时，我当然已经知道答案了，但我无法描述细节。只要知道，终有一天，我会去天堂的草地上迎接你们，在那里，除了兰迪·赛克斯和安妮特·凯尔珀之外，我会很高兴地拥抱你们，叙叙旧。

00:52:48.570 --> 00:53:08.150
<v Sarah Thyre>If my instructions were followed the way I wanted them to be-- see attached instruction envelope number one-- this letter is being read to you from the pulpit of the Simple Shepherd Church of Christ by my best friend, Eileen Mickey-- hi, Eileen-- who is wearing the long-sleeve Lisa Montino designer dress I left behind that always looked so good on me.
<v Sarah Thyre>如果我的指示得到了执行——见附件一号指示信封——这封信现在正由我最好的朋友艾琳·米基从简朴牧羊人基督教会的讲坛上读给你们听——嘿，艾琳——她身上穿的是我留下的那件丽莎·蒙蒂诺长袖设计师连衣裙，那件穿在我身上一直很好看的裙子。

00:53:08.150 --> 00:53:25.970
<v Sarah Thyre>Eileen, I hope you either lost some weight or took it out some on the sides, or you're not going to be able to breathe. Also remember, it needs to be dry cleaned. I know how you and your family love to skimp. But please, don't listen to what anyone says about Woolite. Dry clean.
<v Sarah Thyre>艾琳，我希望你要么瘦了点，要么把侧面改大了，不然你肯定穿不进去。还有记住，它需要干洗。我知道你和你家人有多喜欢省钱。但求你们，别听信任何人关于 Woolite 洗衣液的鬼话。干洗。

00:53:25.970 --> 00:53:43.880
<v Sarah Thyre>Most of you are probably wondering why I did it. You're whispering, "Why, Lord? Why take Trish Moody? Trish was a ray of bright sunshine, always so up and perky and full of love. Pretty, too. Just as smart and sweet and pretty as they come."
<v Sarah Thyre>你们大多数人可能都在纳闷我为什么这么做。你们私语道：“为什么，上帝啊？为什么要带走翠西·穆迪？翠西是那样的阳光，总是那么乐观活泼，充满爱心。长得也漂亮。聪明、甜美、漂亮，无可挑剔。”

00:53:43.880 --> 00:54:25.980
<v Sarah Thyre>You're probably shaking your heads and thinking there's plenty of people a lot worse than Trish Moody. There's her former excuse for a boyfriend, Randy Sykes, for example. The boyfriend who, after Trish accidentally backed her car over his dog, practically beat her senseless. He beat her with words, but still, it might as well have been with his fists. He struck her again and again with words and names such as "manipulative," "jealous," "childish," and others I wouldn't justify in print. The dog's death was a tragic accident, but perhaps also a blessing in disguise, as Randy tended to spend entirely too much time with it.
<v Sarah Thyre>你们可能摇着头觉得翠西·穆迪远不是最坏的人。比如说她以前那个叫兰迪·赛克斯的前男友。那个翠西不小心开车压死他的狗后，几乎把她打得半死的男朋友。他是用语言打的，但就算那样，也和拳头没两样。他用“操纵”、“嫉妒”、“幼稚”等词汇反复抨击她，这些词我甚至不屑写出来。那只狗的死是一场悲剧，但也许是一种因祸得福，因为兰迪花在它身上的时间实在是太多了。

00:54:25.980 --> 00:54:46.300
<v Sarah Thyre>What did Trish's mother say when her daughter, heartbroken over her breakup with Randy, came to her in search of love and understanding? If you're looking for sympathy, you can find it between "sob story" and "syphilis" in the dictionary. Perhaps my mother can live with slogans such as this. I know I can't.
<v Sarah Thyre>当她的女儿翠西因为和兰迪分手伤心欲绝，向她寻求爱和理解时，翠西的母亲说了什么？“如果你想找同情，字典里‘苦情故事’（sob story）和‘梅毒’（syphilis）中间有的是。” 也许我妈能靠这种口号生活，但我不能。

00:54:46.300 --> 00:55:05.260
<v Sarah Thyre>Neither can I live surrounded by friends such as Annette Kelper. Poor, chubby Annette Kelper, who desperately tries to pretend that nobody notices the fact that she's balding on top of her head. That's right. Look closely. Balding, just like a man.
<v Sarah Thyre>我也不能忍受生活在像安妮特·凯尔珀这样的朋友身边。可怜、臃肿的安妮特·凯尔珀，她极力装作没人注意到她头顶正在秃顶的事实。没错，仔细看。秃顶，就像个男人一样。

00:55:05.260 --> 00:55:41.230
<v Sarah Thyre>Perhaps Randy feels sorry for chrome-dome Annette. Maybe that's why he was seen twice in her company in a single five-day period. Seen standing together in the parking lot of the Burger Tabernacle-- her home away from home-- and seen huddled together, laughing on the escalator of the Crabtree Valley Mall. Annette, my supposed best friend, who secretly wanted and coveted everything I owned. Is everyone on earth as two-faced as Annette Kelper? Is everyone as cruel as Randy Sykes? I think not.
<v Sarah Thyre>也许兰迪同情秃头安妮特。也许这就是为什么他在五天内两次被看见和她在一起。被看见一起站在“汉堡会幕”（Burger Tabernacle）的停车场——她的第二个家——还被看见在螃蟹谷商场的自动扶梯上缩在一起大笑。安妮特，我那所谓的最好的朋友，暗中想要并觊觎我拥有的一切。世上每个人都像安妮特·凯尔珀一样虚伪吗？每个人都像兰迪·赛克斯一样残忍吗？我不觉得。

00:55:41.230 --> 00:56:03.890
<v Sarah Thyre>Most of you, the loved ones I left behind, are simple, devoted people. I urge you now to take a look around the room. Are Randy Sykes and Annette sitting in the audience? Are they shifting uncomfortably in the pew, shielding their faces with the 8 and 1/2 by 11 photograph of me I had reproduced to serve as a memento of this occasion?
<v Sarah Thyre>你们大多数人，我留下的亲人，都是单纯、忠诚的人。我请求你们现在环顾四周。兰迪·赛克斯和安妮特在观众席里吗？他们是不是坐在长椅上局促不安，用我为了这个场合制作的 8.5 x 11 大照片遮着脸？

00:56:03.890 --> 00:56:30.340
<v Sarah Thyre>Eileen, read this part real fast before they have a chance to leave. Randy Sykes' drip is the size of my little finger, and that's when it's hard. And I'm not counting the nail-- just the finger. He had sex two times with a boy at Camp Ticonderoga when he was in junior high school. Maybe that explains why he loves it with somebody sticks their finger up his [? couvade. ?] He used to beg me to do that, but I refused. I said, "No way, Randy." He used to do it to himself all the time. That's why I never held hands with him. His hands stink.
<v Sarah Thyre>艾琳，快读这一段，别让他们有机会离开。兰迪·赛克斯的小弟弟只有我的小指头那么大，而且是在硬起来的时候。而且我还没算指甲——就只算手指。他在初中时曾在蒂康德罗加营地和男孩发生过两次性行为。也许这就解释了为什么他喜欢别人把手指插进他的 [? couvade ?] 里。他过去常求我那么做，但我拒绝了。我说：“没门，兰迪。” 他自己老那么做。这就是为什么我从不牵他的手。他的手臭死了。

00:56:30.340 --> 00:56:47.690
<v Sarah Thyre>He secretly thinks he looks like Marlon Brando, but take a good look. A young Marlin Perkins is more like it. Maybe that's what he sees in Annette Kelper. He's an animal lover. She used to come to my house crying. Her breath smelled a mile off, like her uncle's drip. She said he forced her, but that's a lie, because you don't force whores. And that's what she is, a whore.
<v Sarah Thyre>他私下里觉得他长得像马龙·白兰度，但请仔细看看。更像年轻时的马林·珀金斯。也许这就是他在安妮特·凯尔珀身上看到的。他是个动物爱好者。她曾到我家哭诉。她的口臭隔一里地都能闻到，像她叔叔的口臭一样。她说他是强迫她的，那是谎话，因为你不会强迫妓女。而她就是个妓女。

00:56:47.690 --> 00:57:01.060
<v Sarah Thyre>Annette and Randy deserve each other. Drip-Breath and Stinky-Finger, riding up and down the escalator at Crabtree Valley, up and down, up and down. Fancy little shuck-cutlets. Look at them. Take a good, hard look at them. It's their fault I'm dead. They are to blame.
<v Sarah Thyre>安妮特和兰迪天生一对。口臭和臭手，在螃蟹谷的扶梯上上下下，上上下下。这两个时髦的小贱人。看看他们。好好地、狠狠地看看他们。我死了是他们的错。他们才是该受谴责的人。

00:57:01.060 --> 00:57:28.420
<v Sarah Thyre>I urge you now to take those paperweights and stone them. Release your anger. The Bible says it's all right to cast the first stone if someone dead is telling you do it. And I'm telling you now. Pretend the paperweights are stones and cast them upon the guilty. I put aside my savings to pay for the damages to the walls and windows. It's money I was saving for my wedding, and there's plenty of it. So throw. Hurt them the way they hurt me. Kill them! Nobody will hold you responsible. Kill them!
<v Sarah Thyre>我请求你们现在拿起那些镇纸，砸向他们。释放你们的怒火吧。圣经说，如果死人叫你砸，你可以扔出第一块石头。我现在就在叫你们扔。把镇纸想象成石头，砸向那些罪人。我存了钱赔偿墙壁和窗户的损失。那是原本留给我结婚用的钱，多的是。所以扔吧。像他们伤害我一样伤害他们。杀了他们！没人会追究你们。杀了他们！

00:57:28.420 --> 00:58:12.000
<v Sarah Thyre>Eileen, I'm going to allow a few minutes here because it might take a while for certain people to get into the swing of it. Pop in the cassette marked "Stoning," and wait until both Randy and Annette are lifeless. Wait until everyone is finished with their paperweights. And then I want you to hand the microphone over to my mother. Watch the way she trembles and stutters, and remember every gesture as if you were me.
<v Sarah Thyre>艾琳，我会留几分钟，因为某些人可能需要一点时间进入状态。放上标记着“乱石”的磁带，等着兰迪和安妮特都没了气息。等大家用完镇纸。然后我要你把话筒交给我的母亲。看着她颤抖和结巴的样子，记住每一个动作，就像你就是我。

00:58:12.000 --> 00:58:17.530
<v Ira Glass>832-3160. Shirley Jahad? Well, I guess you're not there.
<v Ira Glass>832-3160。雪莉·贾哈德？好吧，我想你不在了。

00:58:17.530 --> 00:58:19.230
<v Shirley Jahad>I'm here, Ira.
<v Shirley Jahad>我在这儿，艾拉。

00:58:19.230 --> 00:58:19.750
<v Ira Glass>You are?
<v Ira Glass>你在？

00:58:19.750 --> 00:58:21.540
<v Shirley Jahad>You know, our volunteers are so resourceful.
<v Shirley Jahad>你知道，我们的志愿者太聪明了。

00:58:21.540 --> 00:58:23.440
<v Ira Glass>And are they getting phone calls in?
<v Ira Glass>他们在接电话吗？

00:58:23.440 --> 00:58:28.180
<v Shirley Jahad>They are taking your phone calls now. 312-832-3160.
<v Shirley Jahad>他们正在接你们的电话。312-832-3160。

00:58:28.180 --> 00:58:38.230
<v Ira Glass>OK, you can get that story, which was from David Sedaris' book Barrel Fever , read by Sarah Thyre, plus also lots of other David Sedaris work, a 90-minute cassette full of all sorts of stuff.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，你可以得到那个故事，来自戴维·塞达里斯的《桶热》（Barrel Fever），莎拉·泰尔朗读，加上许多其他戴维·塞达里斯的作品，一盘 90 分钟的磁带，装满了各种素材。

00:58:38.230 --> 00:58:42.350
<v Shirley Jahad>For just $60. 312-832-3160.
<v Shirley Jahad>只需 60 美元。312-832-3160。

00:58:42.350 --> 00:58:52.860
<v Ira Glass>Or the two CDs that we've been plugging all hour. I should also mention that David is going to be here in Chicago, live this coming Saturday night at the Park West for Milly's Orchid Show. You might want to go see him there.
<v Ira Glass>或者我们整个小时都在宣传的两张 CD。我还得说，戴维这周六晚将亲临芝加哥，在公园西剧场参加“米莉的兰花秀”。你们可能想去看看他。

00:58:52.860 --> 00:58:53.420
<v Shirley Jahad>Cool.
<v Shirley Jahad>太棒了。

00:58:53.420 --> 00:58:53.940
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:58:53.940 --> 00:58:57.790
<v Shirley Jahad>312-832-3160. Call now and what, Ira?
<v Shirley Jahad>312-832-3160。现在打电话，然后呢，艾拉？

00:58:57.790 --> 00:59:04.030
<v Ira Glass>And pledge your support. Remember, this is the only time you're going to get during the drive to get most of these premia and super size it.
<v Ira Glass>认捐支持。记住，这是你在筹款期间能获得大部分赠品并“超值升级”的唯一机会。

00:59:04.030 --> 00:59:05.210
<v Shirley Jahad>Super size it.
<v Shirley Jahad>“超值升级”。

00:59:05.210 --> 00:59:16.060
<v Shirley Jahad>[FUNDING CREDITS]
<v Shirley Jahad>[节目资金来源声明]

00:59:16.060 --> 00:59:31.390
<v Ira Glass>Today's program was produced by Peter Clowney and myself with Dolores Wilber, Alix Spiegel, and Nancy Updike, contributing editors Jack Hitt, Margy Rochlin, and Paul Tough. Paul Tough did the fabulous original interview with Bob featured in the first half of this program. WBEZ management oversight by Torey Malatia.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目由彼得·克劳尼和我自己制作，多洛雷斯·威尔伯、阿利克斯·斯皮格尔和南希·厄普戴克协助，杰克·希特、玛吉·罗克林和保罗·塔夫担任特约编辑。保罗·塔夫进行了节目上半部分那个关于鲍勃的精彩采访。WBEZ 管理层监管由托里·马拉蒂亚负责。

00:59:31.390 --> 00:59:42.680
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[鸣谢]

00:59:42.680 --> 00:59:47.680
<v Ira Glass>To buy a tape of this or other Playhouse shows, call us at WBEZ, 832-3380, or email us, [email protected] . We broadcast proudly from WBEZ Chicago. We'll be back next week with more stories of This American Life. See you then.
<v Ira Glass>欲购买本期或其他剧场的磁带，请拨打 WBEZ：832-3380，或电邮至 [email protected] 。我们从芝加哥 WBEZ 自豪地播出。下周我们将带着更多《美国生活》（This American Life）的故事回归。到时候见。
